From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Tue, 9 Aug 94 14:19:31 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-97] Do credentials have value in a free society?

> I'm interested in minimizing the need for credentials.  If anyone has a way for  
> an extropian society to function entirely without them, please let me know; I  
> will be most impressed.
> 
> -Pete

Distributed, voluntary credentials are called "reputations," and there's a
lot of mumbling amongst Privately Produced Law enthusiasts, crypto-
anarchists, and agoric computing folks about "reputation systems." 

Basically, that just means systems for more officially getting
summaries of opinions of people you trust on the trustworthiness of
others.

When people are talking about crypto protocols for this stuff, you
may see the word "credential" used just to mean some sort of digital
signed statement about somebody.

There's a technique called "blind signatures" that let you prove that
someone said something about you without revealing who you are.
If that were the normal way to do things like credit checks and so
on, then it would be possible to make the sorts of reassurances
that people want about each other, while reducing the ability that
exists now to amass and correllate information about people.

