From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
Date: Tue, 9 Aug 1994 17:10:20 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-8-99] PHIL: a disagreement (maybe) about relativism

I agree with most of what Reilly said, except for the following aside:

> Rationality has
> historically always led to relativism, then reason is abandoned and relativistic
> ideas are held dogmatically.

I've heard you rail aginst 'relativism' before, and am not sure what
you are attacking here, as you usually lump it in with a bunch of
other undesirable traits.

Relativism is the opposite of dogmatism; it is the knowledge that no
meme-complex, simply by existing, has a privileged claim on your
reality.  You may argue that this is not the case, in which case I 
would like to know which memes have that privilege so I can watch 
out for them.

This is not to say that we (defined as 'you and me', 'extropians',
'humans', or anything else) don't agree that we _want_ to spread a
certain set of memes -- I simply do not want to mistake our stamp of
approval for a definition of an Absolute, and therefore of 'objective
reality', which is not a useful concept because it is by definition
not knowable.  There are degrees of certainty, and being 100% certain
is equal to faith, which must be distinguished from expectation lest
we slip into dogmatism.

Personally, I think the problem is that once rationality leads to
relativism, the map ends, because there is no road on any map
marked "Your Future" -- and a lot of people spend a lot of time 
looking for it, not finding it, and wondering why.

// g






