From: "Scott H. James" <sjames@emmy.UMD.EDU>
Date: Mon, 8 Aug 1994 15:56:12 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-776169971] Uploading, Cryonics and the Self

              On Uploading, Cryonics and the Self

                       (by TMP)


The Introduction to the Question:

In recent discussions on uploading, cryonics and self-preservation
there appears to be an implicit assumption made which I
believe is not obvious and worthy of some heavy contemplation
(speaking from personal experience at least :-)).  This (as yet
unstated) assumption had been debated heavily at one time amongst the
Extropians.  (Richard Kennaway posted a nice summary of this debate on
the essay list in December 1992. If the general audience would like to
review it I could repost if so desired).  As this debate apparently
has been forgotten and more importantly, because I believe I can shed
new light on it, I will henceforth restate the problem in my own words
and toss a new rock of opinion into the larger river of Truth.

The Difficulties Inherent:

Perform the following thought experiment: Take a brain from a sentient
being of your choice (say Fred).  Switch same brain to the `off'
position through your favorite method i.e. sleep, cryonic suspension,
really good drugs etc...  Now, insert brain into any of the ACME Brain
Duplicator product line (the exact technology involved is currently a
company secret).  Run the Brain Duplicator twice and remove the
resultant output pair of brains.  Label the original brain (i.e. the
switched off Fred) with a name tag, combine with duplicate brains,
juggle all three in the air for two minutes and place each on a table
under their own coconut half-shell.  Shuffle coconuts until
sufficiently confused, then, with coconuts still face down on the
table, turn all brains to the `on' position.  Invite family members,
friends and experts in various professions (at least one telephone
repairman) to analyze and query the coconuts to determine which of
them contains the original sentient being, Fred.  Have the coconuts
give a panel discussion on who they think is the original Fred.
Finally, lift up the coconuts, show which coconut contains the
original Fred and try to convince the other brains that they are not
(Fred, that is).

The problem is that if ACME did their job well enough, it is
conceivable that no external entity could possibly detect which brain
was the original Fred.  Moreover, at the moment of turning the brains
`on' each brain would have every memory, every experience and have
every reason to believe that it was indeed Fred, even if it is
informed by the rest of the known universe that it is not.  So, in
what sense is the original Fred STILL the original Fred?  To put it
another way, if the label fell off, then who would know for sure?

Try a different experiment.  Suppose we take a `slow' upload path i.e.
start with Fred and keep on making modifications to Fred until we end
up with, let's say, CyberFred.  Only, let us do the same thing as
before.  That is to say, suppose that the Uploader, instead of simply
making irreversible changes to Fred, makes a backup of Fred before
making each modification.  Suppose the Uploader messes up and goes to
a backup to continue their work.  Do they kill Fred or not, or do they
only kill Fred if they are SUCCESSFUL at a modification?

Let's make things even more ridiculous. Suppose that other Uploaders
take the Fred backup, make their own backups and make their own
hierarchies of Fred's.  This continues until we have a very large
laboratory full of Fred's, each a little different than the other,
some maybe `working', some maybe not `working', some who knows
if they are working or not.  Maybe, when the Uploaders are feeling a
little wild they start splicing pieces of different Fred clones to other
Fred clones.  Again, we ask the same question: where is Fred?  Does
the fact that electrons flow in and out of all of these brains provide its
form of `enhancement'?  What if the electrons go back and forth between
the Freds?  Can one be a partial-Fred?

An Answer Which May Not Be Very Satisfying:

In its most simpleminded sense, I presume one could say that in the
first case Fred is still Fred because it was the original. Period.  In
the second case, only integral branches of Fred could be allowed.  One
could also deny the existence of any duplicating technology on first
principles, for instance: Heisenberg's uncertainty principle does not
allow us to duplicate ANYTHING exactly; Goedel's Theorem shows us that
the patterns of our own thoughts are beyond our own comprehension (or
at least complete comprehension ... unless we are inconsistent to
begin with, now wait a minute ....); and/or The Law of Life states
that Biology is just too damn hard.  One could argue that it is
impossible to turn any brain, and in particular Fred's brain
COMPLETELY off because Fred is actually a running process (as opposed
to a program).  One could also make counter proposals to these.
However, I think this whole debate misses the main point (or at least
the one I'm trying to make) which is:

    `Fred' does NOT exist as a continuous time object

`Fred' is the operation of a set of physical components at a
particular instant in time (or the limit of a set of operations if you
like the Calculus thing). The body of Fred may be continuous (sort of,
in pieces at least), the ideas Fred has are continuous in that they
are modified by time (and even time may not be continuous if you like
to really get weird) but `Fred' itself is not an unending strand of
existence.  Fred can be born.  Fred can be broken into pieces.  Fred can
be copied.  Fred can be altered.  Fred can be branched.  Fred can be
killed. Fred only exists INSTANTANEOUSLY.

To be sure, this is not a terribly original idea -- refer to your
favorite metaphysical religious texts for similar statements
(St. Augustine, for instance was so upset about this that he became a
Christian.  See _The Confessions_).  However, this concept does shed
a rather strange light on idea of `self-preservation' ...

The difficulty is that we are beings that are caught in a time line
and consequently the idea of instantaneous existence is counter
intuitive.  We LIKE to think of ourselves as immortal, as a continuous
time line going off into the future and receding into the past.
The concept of continuity gives us security and indeed our very
identity.  In some sense it is impossible to think otherwise (but I'm
giving it a go anyway :-)).  Our instantaneous existence has a memory of
the past, hope (trepidation) for the future, pain (pleasure) for the
moment, and, moreover, all things appear to indicate these memories,
hopes and pain will persist for a pretty long time (or at least some
non-zero interval of time).  I believe, however, that if one takes a
materialistic stance of the universe (which I certainly do) one is
forced into the situation I have shown (NOTE: one could also NOT take
such a materialistic stance and allow say the Spirit of Fred to exist
outside our universe but running parallel to it, made of components we
cannot manipulate by definition.  If you believe such things ...
enjoy!).

The kicker here is the PURPOSE for exercising, eating vitamins,
raising a family, climbing mountains, reserving a slot at Alcor and in
general striving for immortality becomes a little nebulous.  If you
only exist instantaneously, what is it that you are trying to
preserve?  To quote a piece of the compilation by Richard Kennaway
(cited above):

   "Uploading, duplication, and modification of persons makes clear what
the Buddhists have known for thousands of years, that personal identity is
a convenient, conventional fiction.  There is not really any such thing.
But if so, why should one be motivated to continue living at all?"

Some Possibly Irrelevant Thoughts Which Might Help:

In the beginning of this particular planet much of the evolution of
life was based on the workings of an organism whose direct ancestors
live today as the cyanobacteria or the blue-green algae.  As this creature
evolved to live in the Precambrian air, the evolving bacteria existed
as a mass of organisms growing on top of each other in puddles of
sea water.  Only the bacteria on the top of the mound was alive at any
one time; all others below were dead by necessity.  In the process,
the cellular structure of the cyanobacteria evolved to higher (:-))
life forms.  The resultant surviving stacks of compacted dead
cells are known today as stromatolites.

As the world, rife with Hardware Technology, Wetware Technology,
Religions, and War hurtles toward the Catastrophe (or the
Singularity, if you prefer.  Catastrophe theory came first though you
know (bow to Thom)) and we as Extropians grasp for our bit of
immortality, perhaps it would help, if just for sanity, to ponder
these organisms.  Remember that we as Individuals also exist only
as part of larger Stromatolite, living lives that are Year to Year,
Day to Day, Moment to Moment -- just as the larger Stromatolite itself
exists from Year to Year, Day to Day, Moment to Moment.  And
as we strive to control whatever part of our destiny we feel we are
able to control, perhaps a Peace of Mind (or piece of Fred) at any
particular moment is all that we really have.

Seize the moment.

  The Mad Penguin (s_james@math.umd.edu)  
  August 7, 1994











