From: cappello@cs.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)
Date: Mon, 8 Aug 94 15:46:54 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-776169973] Do credentials have value in a free society?

>Licensing, in the form you speak of above, is a necessary evil of modern life.

Of course, one hopes that no evil is necessary.  


I believe that the process of credentialing often interferes with learning.  To  
avoid this, these two processes should be separated.  (see  
http://www.cs.ucsb.edu/~cappello/courses/cs22/Philosophy.html for a brief  
explanation of this with respect to a course on C and Unix).  I would love to  
dispense with credentialing entirely, but I also believe that credentialing has  
economic value.

I used to feel that asking for credentials was an admission that one was  
incompetent to judge for one's self.  Now, I have become somewhat comfortable  
with my incompetence.  Specialization happens.  I routinely seek advice in  
matters about which I am not expert.  This is not to say that I am incapable of  
becoming an expert.  Time is money.   On the whole, I think that it is a more  
efficient use of my time to ask a doctor about health matters (perhaps among  
other people) than to ask an electrical engineer.  Surely such discrimination  
is wise.  I will give more weight to a doctor's unsubstantiated opinion about  
health than I will to an electrical engineer's unsubstantiated opinion about  
health.  I will not take the time to learn _everything_ that the doctor knows  
(even if he were willing to communicate the information to me) to judge for  
myself the quality of his information base and his reasoning.  I can do this  
and still take responsibility for any decision I make that is based on his  
opinion.  I don't think that marks me as an uncreative thinker.  Just another  
person with priorities.

I'm interested in minimizing the need for credentials.  If anyone has a way for  
an extropian society to function entirely without them, please let me know; I  
will be most impressed.

-Pete


